---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro"

const APP = import.meta.env.PUBLIC_APP_URL || "https://app.docaru.com"

// The old homepage opened "Every document you own has a deadline. We tell you
// which one is next." That sold the layer on top. Most of what a household
// keeps — a birth certificate, a degree, a land deed — has no deadline at all
// and exists in here so it can be reached from another city. Storage is the
// product; renewals are what makes it worth opening.
const reasons = [
  {
    title: "We cannot read your files",
    body: "Scans are encrypted in your browser before upload, with a key derived from a passphrase that never reaches us. What we store is ciphertext with no filename attached. That is not a promise about our conduct — it is a property of the code, and the code is public."
  },
  {
    title: "Your family's documents, not just yours",
    body: "One vault, everyone in the household. Your mother's citizenship certificate is where you can find it at 2am from another timezone, instead of in a drawer thirty kilometres away."
  },
  {
    title: "Run it yourself, or let us run it",
    body: "One container and a database. Self-hosting is the stronger option and it is free — we would rather say so than pretend our servers are safer than yours."
  },
  {
    title: "It knows which ones expire",
    body: "For the documents that do have deadlines, it works out the date you have to start — counting back through processing times and the prerequisites you have not ordered yet — and reminds you then, not when it expires."
  }
]
---
<Base
  title="Docaru — the documents you will not put in Google Drive"
  description="A self-hosted, browser-encrypted vault for your household's documents, reachable from anywhere — that also tells you when the ones with deadlines need renewing."
  path="/"
>
  <h1>The documents you will not<br />put in Google&nbsp;Drive.</h1>

  <p class="lede">
    Your citizenship certificate, your degree, the land deed, your mother's passport. Encrypted in
    your browser, stored where nobody — including us — can open them, and reachable from wherever
    you happen to be when somebody asks for a copy.
  </p>

  <div class="actions">
    <a class="btn" href={`${APP}/registration/new`}>Create a vault</a>
    <a class="btn btn-quiet" href="/self-host">Or run it yourself</a>
  </div>

  <p class="note">Free for one person and a gigabyte. No card.</p>

  <div class="panel">
    <p class="eyebrow">The problem, concretely</p>
    <p style="margin:0">
      You are in Doha. A visa application wants your birth certificate and your father's citizenship
      certificate. Both are in a folder in a cupboard in Kathmandu. Somebody has to go and find them,
      photograph them badly, and send them over WhatsApp — where they will sit forever, in a chat, on
      a server you do not control.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Why this rather than a cloud drive</h2>

  <div class="cards">
    {reasons.map((reason) => (
      <div class="card">
        <h3>{reason.title}</h3>
        <p class="note">{reason.body}</p>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <h2>The renewal layer</h2>

  <p>
    A calendar reminder says "passport expires 14 March". It does not say "start in November, because
    renewing from abroad takes ten weeks and needs a police certificate you have not ordered yet."
    The failure is not memory. It is lead time.
  </p>

  <p>
    For documents that expire, Docaru computes the date you have to <em>start</em> — from the renewal
    window, the worst-case processing time, and the lead time on every prerequisite — and reminds you
    then. The rules behind it are
    <a href="https://github.com/cdrrazan/Docaru/tree/main/rules">a public dataset</a>, so nobody has
    to trust us about a number they can check themselves.
  </p>

  <div class="actions">
    <a class="btn btn-quiet" href={`${APP}/when-to-start`}>Work out when to start &rarr;</a>
    <a class="btn btn-quiet" href={`${APP}/playbooks`}>Browse the playbooks</a>
  </div>

  <p class="note">
    Both are free and need no account. Reminders are never paywalled either — putting the safety
    feature behind a card form would poison the trust the whole thing rests on.
  </p>
</Base>
